From 9fc78326faae24702ccb7b982bbb46ede96dc570 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 13:08:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] nouveau,compiler: build the Rust half without a
 standard library
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

NAK, NIL and the shared `compiler` crate are written against `std`, and
a tier-3 target may not have one. aarch64-nintendo-switch-freestanding
is such a target: its built-in spec says `"std": false`, and Rust's
`horizon` support in std is the 3DS's, reached through a unix PAL this
target does not have — a std built for it would select
`sys/pal/unsupported`, where the operations that make std worth having
return errors. So the crates are built #![no_std] against core and
alloc instead.

Almost all of it is spelling. Of the ~210 `std::` paths in these
crates, the overwhelming majority are core under another name —
std::mem (50), std::cmp (32), std::slice (30), std::ops (22),
std::marker (20) — and the owning containers are alloc's. What actually
needed an operating system was seven things, and each is replaced by
something that does not:

  std::env::var("NAK_DEBUG")     os_get_option(), Mesa's own accessor
                                 for exactly this, now allowlisted in
                                 NAK's bindings
  std::sync::OnceLock<Debug>     an AtomicU32 with one value reserved
                                 for "not read yet". Debug is a single
                                 u32 of flag bits, so a race
                                 recomputes the same answer
  std::panic::catch_unwind (x2)  called directly: it cannot catch
                                 anything on a target whose panic
                                 strategy is abort, which this one sets
  std::collections::HashMap      hashbrown, which is the implementation
                                 std itself uses, with rustc-hash's
                                 FxBuildHasher. rustc-hash defines its
                                 Fx aliases only under its `std`
                                 feature, so they are reconstructed
  std::fs/std::io in             #[cfg(feature = "std")]. It is dead
  save_graphviz                  code with three commented-out call
                                 sites and the only filesystem use in
                                 NAK
  std::io::Result over           a local error type. No Rust code here
  u_memstream                    reads or writes anything; what std::io
                                 supplied was a Result alias and a way
                                 to say "the C call failed"
  eprintln! (21 sites)           the same write(2) std ends up in

Two float methods, f32::round and f32::powf/log2, live on std's float
types rather than in core, because core has no libm. newlib supplies
roundf, powf and log2f and the driver already links -lm, so they are
called directly rather than pulling in a Rust libm for three calls.

Generated crate roots are generated with #![no_std] too, and bindgen is
asked for --use-core so the bindings name C's integer types as
::core::ffi::* rather than ::std::os::raw::*.

NAK's bindings no longer drag in the kernel-mode winsys. nak_bindings.h
included nouveau_bo.h, nouveau_context.h, nouveau_device.h, xf86drm.h
and drm-uapi/nouveau_drm.h for one file — nak/hw_runner.rs, which is
behind #[cfg(test)] — and that made NAK unbuildable wherever the winsys
is not, over symbols nothing that would be built uses. The include and
the allowlist entries it feeds are now asked for only with_tests.

Finally, the crate layout. Rust requires exactly one
#[global_allocator] and one #[panic_handler] per linked artefact and
emits both into every --crate-type staticlib. Mesa builds NAK and NIL
as two staticlibs, which works with std because std supplies the shim
and the archive member holding it is simply not pulled a second time.
Without std each archive defines the shim beside the code that uses it,
and the two collide:

  ld: libb.a(...): multiple definition of `__rustc::__rust_alloc';
      liba.a(...): first defined here
  ... and __rust_dealloc, __rust_realloc, __rust_alloc_zeroed,
      rust_begin_unwind

Measured with and without -O, so it is not a codegen-unit accident.
Where there is no std, NAK and NIL are therefore rlibs and
src/nouveau/rust_runtime is the one staticlib: it adds no code, names
the two crates so the linker keeps them, and supplies the allocator
(over the C library's memalign/free, so there is one heap rather than
two) and the panic handler (abort, which is what the target's panic
strategy already means).

Whether std exists is read from host_machine.kernel(), which is the
same property Meson's own Rust compiler uses to decide whether to
sanity-check a `fn main()` or a `#![no_std] #![no_main]` program.
Everything above is a no-op where that says a standard library exists.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 4 — the nouveau Vulkan driver for Horizon
Why: aarch64-nintendo-switch-freestanding has no prebuilt std, and
  building one would produce the `unsupported` PAL
  (docs/rust-toolchain.md § 6).
Evidence: `meson compile` for aarch64/horizon with
  -Dvulkan-drivers=nouveau produced 500+ rustc errors that were one
  fact — with no std there is no prelude:
    141 error: cannot find attribute `derive` in this scope
    102 error[E0405]: cannot find trait `Default` in this scope
      2 error[E0463]: can't find crate for `std`
  With this change NAK, NIL, compiler, nvidia_headers, bitview,
  nak_latencies and both bindgen crates all compile, and
  libnouveau_rust_runtime.a is 15 292 744 bytes with 0 R_AARCH64_TLS
  relocations and exactly one definition of each allocator and panic
  symbol.
Upstream: partly. The core::/alloc:: spelling, the dead-code gate, the
  bindings/winsys separation and --use-core are improvements anywhere.
  The one-staticlib arrangement is conditional on a platform property
  and changes nothing where std exists.
